#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133892 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133708 :question/id #uuid "7417f4d8-262c-5d16-9b35-f1c9710345e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9513" :question/problem "![](images/74/74033bfe17cefe62d5306b2186b9816ba26b816ece10c5dab5178abe1a9aaf89.png)\n\nFind the definite integral equivalent to the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_C x^2 \\, \\textrm d y,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $25x^2 + 4y^2 = 100,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,5),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 5\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}=4{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =20{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0712bb6-c0f8-5551-8362-ae23629aa618" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "091b6b7e-813c-5b73-865c-f669e169c171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-6t+9)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256862} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2d80f4-0dad-59e0-a83c-87ee7e93fd9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-6t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257567} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb0bf06-323d-5f15-8bf3-af2b48419f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-6t+9)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258045} {:answer/id #uuid "c51659c0-3351-506b-83b7-da83cb4dac4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+6t+9)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258505} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3799de-5093-52dd-b475-702c04f8913f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-6t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a2d80f4-0dad-59e0-a83c-87ee7e93fd9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-6t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257567} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc17d7b-1584-5ada-831d-f64a488b2cef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259142}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9417c70-141e-5010-bf56-db9beb8a2e51" :question/math-academy-id "q-124121" :question/problem "![](images/36/36b985c811eb28d8220017c6b7407b3a104554d785dafdb35f17553c262bdc35.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,-2)$ to the point $(2,4),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,6t-2⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(2,4).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(-2+t(4-(-2)))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,6t-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)\\cdot (6t-2) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-12t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(18{t}^{2}-12t+2)\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-6t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d42eaa5c-5f6b-510e-bdcf-2b464beb3986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}(\\sin t + \\cos^{2} t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130673} {:answer/id #uuid "d7026333-b868-5b77-8572-6720d98e9aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sin t\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130674} {:answer/id #uuid "dba80d00-022e-5a28-8a5b-4519c7470885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sin t\\cos^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130675} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1a44ec-2dfb-5482-a31a-d95ee3f11a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} t\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130676} {:answer/id #uuid "61ab31cf-ee4d-5245-9adb-a50358b808e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}(\\sin t + \\cos t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130677}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0618f5a7-744f-51cb-8881-7064318ea8b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130672} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a01ac03-516d-5191-b62a-df55277746ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369280} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2ea435-9b78-5237-b1de-d27c14d67721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369281} {:answer/id #uuid "340e5763-cb2f-5d3a-ab41-9e4aab2f5062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369282} {:answer/id #uuid "12a066d8-58b4-511c-9cb2-861f5afc7e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369283} {:answer/id #uuid "99c2fa33-7e84-5fa9-b445-20cdce34028b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae2ea435-9b78-5237-b1de-d27c14d67721" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369281} :answer-field/id #uuid "11a352f7-9105-5249-b023-dd67c620d14d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369279}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1880828-25c5-5042-afdb-e835de5c511c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124283" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f83043b44a8ad9181e66de57357397deb49c4b0e79b5e6e16330305a58391148.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(4,0)$ to the point $(-4,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259645 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cb2f45-a0a2-5f1e-bf8e-2270b4bdb803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{t}^{2}-t+7)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257213} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4bbd3a-3496-5b69-bcf9-e8a2d9e5408a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(18{t}^{2}+t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257577} {:answer/id #uuid "66a80b9e-0979-58cf-8713-06c17381084c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257688} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0a3d3c-41b5-5b9c-8564-d3b85a1aaff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{t}^{2}+5)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257751} {:answer/id #uuid "84976c13-5b58-592e-9b5b-401ce06fe5c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{t}^{2}+7t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66a80b9e-0979-58cf-8713-06c17381084c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257688} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8fa6e0a-e87c-5a7d-8db9-0d8ef84a9586" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259328}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0410ccfc-9f5a-5741-9bf7-f1f601669aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-124270" :question/problem "![](images/58/58995f1bc5b45dbe0d30a9d0cbf704d792b955e7195c0e4dc3610ddf2e576579.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,-1)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,2t-1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(-1+t(1-(-1)))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,2t-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)^{2}-(2t-1) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-6t+1-2t+1 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-8t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275d71c4-46a9-5261-9eb4-5c0c8e6b9dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257127} {:answer/id #uuid "6e447aa7-08e1-50c3-91b6-e0971243693d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257753} {:answer/id #uuid "88cbd20a-ccc7-55e9-8402-c84e72ce8a7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257982} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dc8a41-c7c1-5d26-b4f6-2e6bbd735d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258227} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e80ec8-d12c-5842-b5d1-daee11dbe703" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e447aa7-08e1-50c3-91b6-e0971243693d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257753} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d13a439-5bf8-5f5d-9ec6-8ae8c6bdbf6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259275}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7eef0fe8-9d36-5fca-a5de-d79c02a74295" :question/math-academy-id "q-124279" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d55ece1948f9ecb7da7d489b86a297651a34f409f83ba2470f6dd06678a375e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(0,-3)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259654 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f83140-8d60-5e3b-8557-38a73fea7b19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256790} {:answer/id #uuid "130cd1bb-c702-58af-b317-816f00770ad6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256955} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8051c9-5f19-5e7c-9a70-1cd1c77f1c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257745} {:answer/id #uuid "933f36c7-2d77-5384-a819-869eaeb53ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258081} {:answer/id #uuid "b62bc8f9-e700-500d-bcd6-4f8a56698270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d8051c9-5f19-5e7c-9a70-1cd1c77f1c0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257745} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e765f7a-7a63-5189-8586-c70b56370d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259081}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "76e58dcc-8bc1-5f3d-ae09-2c38a29543eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-124287" :question/problem "![](images/69/69a10b5524670d31cfc9f2982e37d58a5ee9ca39fe49f35b9dace011275a635a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(9-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along three-quarters of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(1,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9-(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =9-9{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =9(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }9{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =27{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "756639e5-ef49-5356-874a-b797a567ade2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369267} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f350f1-37ad-503f-9ea6-70c30ae57437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369268} {:answer/id #uuid "cb15b44a-28cb-5de2-b373-a77569fe3933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369269} {:answer/id #uuid "be76a975-6edc-58eb-8a4c-da7fbf2781b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369270} {:answer/id #uuid "68681886-c9c0-5332-b1f7-d6c36341760a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3f350f1-37ad-503f-9ea6-70c30ae57437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369268} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea741151-0b0f-527e-9bc4-e1287cde677a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369266}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0acc8f31-7302-5dee-af54-ae46e10b8d37" :question/math-academy-id "q-124266" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b21c9c11aa3320a729db0d8a71f5c89069d46527e78dfc76b47ae3925387c770.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(3x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-2,2)$ to the point $(-1,1),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨t-2,\\,2-t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,2)$ and $(-1,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-2+t(-1-(-2))),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-2,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3(t-2)+2(2-t) \\\\  & =3t-6+4-2t \\\\  & =t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a8d9450-4800-5112-a544-9e325560e579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387486} {:answer/id #uuid "047d0818-f7ba-51e2-b160-54925fc6a14f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387487} {:answer/id #uuid "029046db-d3c3-5031-8d7b-336fa4ba1219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387488} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdec4ad-6b88-5993-8466-125c2cf0469c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387489} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb35e9d-0c57-5ee2-93e2-209a367b2cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a8d9450-4800-5112-a544-9e325560e579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387486} :answer-field/id #uuid "09c0fb83-a54b-597a-9d51-eaad92361b4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387485}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48afea1b-7419-5b27-97ac-ca8861895f05" :question/math-academy-id "q-124277" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f532544febd248846eede65941f4617d325d0f91fec291d2679ba28abb2fb8fc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc88e3d-a852-5587-8a01-ed034f9f4385" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387493} {:answer/id #uuid "0354e92a-880d-5b37-9463-ad3a8ea451ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387494} {:answer/id #uuid "05b857f2-d2e7-58cf-af80-9a20296d1870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387495} {:answer/id #uuid "5303fcf8-09a5-5b94-af0f-54b031ab7e05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387496} {:answer/id #uuid "29b535f4-dc26-5f10-9f77-d35c710a0661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b857f2-d2e7-58cf-af80-9a20296d1870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387495} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af7ee7b-5d10-5025-8624-92a4d018ecb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387492}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e84b6989-0b06-5564-bfd3-0660ddb2a037" :question/math-academy-id "q-124293" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d02a56e1f927ccf8bd76a6fcc46dec61259649176695912865dd050657ce7f0.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bf68642-1bc2-5ac7-9a05-766d932162e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387500} {:answer/id #uuid "f72d462d-8b4a-5ce9-af5e-b55e5d4bd6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387501} {:answer/id #uuid "64c3b71b-6781-591c-9b16-935e82c7d8d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3){sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387502} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3ffd3c-9264-5a32-8db9-c3420cd55260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387503} {:answer/id #uuid "38807c4a-be0d-5f98-b548-41b11aa72f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3){cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e3ffd3c-9264-5a32-8db9-c3420cd55260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387503} :answer-field/id #uuid "164502c8-6757-5ba5-b73c-b45e5adc8b09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387499}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d9bcf07-4605-5aed-9b42-699d630fbd7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124298" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1956c8291f1bcecd7d37082110861ed7265fa40f7f85cbc69221cfdc5bea2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =12{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t+12({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t+12 \\\\  & =4({sin}^{2}⁡t+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }4({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals With Respect to Y"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133709 :question/id #uuid "e45bef0c-86ba-5030-bb0c-274df22673f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9521" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_C \\left(x^2 + y^2\\right)\\,\\textrm d y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the circle $x^2 + y^2 = 4,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,2),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fc3a36829c9780c34e13f253d607c3849bfd3ef1399a77a79a3dd3d30ef7140.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}y = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b45822-868e-51bb-b551-345739e9ca44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88f1368e-19de-5af6-9081-a2440a11a658" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130649} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3066ea-c1a8-5d75-a8ca-7d6d46b3a4f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130650} {:answer/id #uuid "131472fe-cad4-5fbc-9c40-b3b2c50d5bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130651} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5b5767-e617-5bf6-8860-635461710498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130652} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc20e8b-b52b-5f4e-af69-47c238f31a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130653}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7ab1c901-b048-5ba8-9df2-862e546c596b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130648} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5c27e6-6d15-5543-baca-509a958d1428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369253} {:answer/id #uuid "81b4c97c-6573-5711-9cd1-f51a4a264e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369254} {:answer/id #uuid "4b80e13c-c214-5a76-9bff-a55badcb706d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369255} {:answer/id #uuid "938debfc-b447-5b7c-a524-0c933d4da736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369256} {:answer/id #uuid "977e7ec8-5c46-57e8-b421-0c2f023be513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "977e7ec8-5c46-57e8-b421-0c2f023be513" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369257} :answer-field/id #uuid "29184995-008b-5dbe-af80-c01d22170858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369252}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdf2c9bf-2367-5618-ad0e-1a35e799a6f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124120" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f531f2467284ef84e0af0758a8e8554f1e5f72ea08d30d0b80c83076fbe5d7a3.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,0)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(0+t(1-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t \\\\  & =1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099c51d1-91fb-55ee-987f-bff891e71b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256866} {:answer/id #uuid "36c1045e-ca0b-598a-af18-e80132339c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257262} {:answer/id #uuid "86d6990c-bef3-51f4-9954-5afedefe853c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257965} {:answer/id #uuid "889b3465-0076-578d-8931-4a15986da352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257979} {:answer/id #uuid "fd053697-39b2-5e77-9cf0-6997020d6328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86d6990c-bef3-51f4-9954-5afedefe853c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257965} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94ee333-488f-593d-8e2c-fc868b0959b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259329}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb08f302-8cb5-5978-acbb-0bba1cddced3" :question/math-academy-id "q-124123" :question/problem "![](images/84/8417d2d62e9213ec3f92108495107fc982dc399e52cca8982e01516457f5692a.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40dc00cd-4c24-526f-b0be-714180e4f0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257359} {:answer/id #uuid "5febbd5f-4713-5fff-8f7f-17efac6589cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257622} {:answer/id #uuid "7696a3d2-8e7a-5c22-b23e-1b2752090aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257825} {:answer/id #uuid "ad788cdb-65a4-5dfe-965b-6ccf2a1c8f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258298} {:answer/id #uuid "e855ef6b-a065-56dc-b0dc-28e3c44034ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e855ef6b-a065-56dc-b0dc-28e3c44034ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258795} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ebaf3f-afef-5801-a181-f3c0188294e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259353}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0853e6c9-d856-5aec-b1f2-e5d65484f5a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124167" :question/problem "![](images/55/559a6b6cb717c10e60b77da402e310fc5895c814655b4ca1748eba294d712a32.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{2}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper half of the circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a half of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[(1)^{3}-(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57205941-d594-5f13-9a16-fe0cd389d946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257534} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb566f1-93cb-5af8-a57a-1b33ba0d8412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257733} {:answer/id #uuid "74b195e5-6b2c-5425-b2b5-b8110e1a0731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257803} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c06057-adaf-5508-83fe-4fb2c2b5d5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258478} {:answer/id #uuid "e3249ef7-a202-5faa-95b6-80450e4666cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1c06057-adaf-5508-83fe-4fb2c2b5d5a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258478} :answer-field/id #uuid "124db33d-09bf-50c0-a6e0-28e8a683c661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259065}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "105c34ef-d15b-56de-a1a8-f1b42bfb4fee" :question/math-academy-id "q-124173" :question/problem "![](images/db/dbd3fdd67ac209544717fc7d4b33831c7cdfd9f6839a7c1410f43ffd385d63ff.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}{8y}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(0,-1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}{8y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021c19b6-a87f-59bb-986b-654c56b918a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256802} {:answer/id #uuid "54fdfd50-de07-5cfb-b6d6-270b66947185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257507} {:answer/id #uuid "6abe279c-1db9-56ef-99fe-6e272def1150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257723} {:answer/id #uuid "debdbff0-5139-5a2f-ac8e-84956824c0b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258722} {:answer/id #uuid "f66ec332-aeec-5f7f-8bb0-93630c7f3749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6abe279c-1db9-56ef-99fe-6e272def1150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257723} :answer-field/id #uuid "4358961a-328e-53e0-ad60-06b7463ba396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259155}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd888ac9-9398-5cd7-abd9-7da37aaa7d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-124175" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab95b870025708090e40cbd7645072e8f4453cf50047b2a00036a30171c81994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(\\sqrt{x-1}+y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}-t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ceacbe-c767-5fbf-a196-9dc0a6e3ef27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256975} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2a09aa-6780-57b9-84e1-0804ea1c0959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257023} {:answer/id #uuid "37384247-4488-580e-ab77-fc38b0f10247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257266} {:answer/id #uuid "3999c92c-0ae4-51a9-9073-6aeee6da3e4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257290} {:answer/id #uuid "7eedef54-bfcb-5d25-a867-579313c708bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eedef54-bfcb-5d25-a867-579313c708bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257914} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea48476d-dc43-5cf6-bf5c-5a8d7d48b4f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259442}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f008788-4322-589f-b4f5-5bd6b468b9b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124177" :question/problem "![](images/27/272b0afabca579f65cbaa7949fd1be70427ff0fd860eb953207bbc8eda7b6e9c.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{3x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-left quarter of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-3,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-9[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb77b4b-9f0a-5ae7-b350-e9c8ee7ac7f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257019} {:answer/id #uuid "50f9dd65-239e-59ad-8a3d-93b2debc6b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257473} {:answer/id #uuid "6b988f18-d213-513d-9832-3e63e11d42ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257732} {:answer/id #uuid "bf507ac3-4f74-5b49-a090-ea632c0a57d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258450} {:answer/id #uuid "f63874ba-42bf-5ec0-a115-516773880a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf507ac3-4f74-5b49-a090-ea632c0a57d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258450} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4362a76-2e86-5ca7-8502-9a0cc1964b8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259430}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cdf631e5-2841-5a67-a8ff-d0eebc8b7e83" :question/math-academy-id "q-124212" :question/problem "![](images/df/dfa95100566fce9ab113b00ae53d1a76e8977b68aa4bac0e9b6892a9930b70d4.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the lower half of the circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{3}}{8(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ] \\\\  & =-8(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f7b9b1-6205-5e9a-abd6-d1dfcd93b57c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256979} {:answer/id #uuid "614bd43e-4103-5afd-b258-686e690c6a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257637} {:answer/id #uuid "ac79318b-520f-5618-92c3-a94976c39773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258284} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e76e62-2425-5791-b451-5fd5063d747e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258479} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5e0345-52da-5c07-878f-0a4c916fd920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd5e0345-52da-5c07-878f-0a4c916fd920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258996} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e95fb8c-bf91-5786-bbd2-5640b44aefed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259060}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1879edf5-1344-5830-a3f2-7e9af982bae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124217" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb4c4828626255e26e298a0cdc202b37852df9d39bcfe15e0e67d350d90f774a.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(2,2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(2,2).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2+t(2-2)),\\,(0+t(2-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})+{x}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (2t)}{2})+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+4)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4+\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(4-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )-(0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)] \\\\  & =2[4-(-1)+1] \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57321071-ed92-5969-b645-38b2e65f944b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257537} {:answer/id #uuid "62251978-7b64-514f-956c-3adbb9ea5ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257647} {:answer/id #uuid "6c038848-0fc0-55d3-b62d-414a628f5e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}(e+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257735} {:answer/id #uuid "707e588a-fbf2-5a85-9ad2-4cf1abd166f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257768} {:answer/id #uuid "de648ba3-07ad-5cb0-a4a0-5469d13b5ff7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62251978-7b64-514f-956c-3adbb9ea5ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257647} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae7cf10-da4c-5368-b5cf-081f5c11280f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259305}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11aa222d-e9ea-5ecd-ab97-032f5281bcab" :question/math-academy-id "q-124273" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e5257867dae67451500793687a2da24ee31a73a17c691d8d8a6dcded46e8993.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the three-quarters of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(3,0),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={cos}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-9{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}[{e}^{1}-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce947fb0-2af2-5578-bc88-f97fd8bc5195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369274} {:answer/id #uuid "7faeed0c-8c4d-51f9-816f-430b26355ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369275} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4e0df-ee12-54ca-90c1-55fd453aaffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369276} {:answer/id #uuid "17c11c14-5bd8-5dfc-9bc8-39959c08d204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369277} {:answer/id #uuid "9230ca12-f5f1-5580-9077-a7bbc7def856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7faeed0c-8c4d-51f9-816f-430b26355ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369275} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f91420-1dd1-57a3-bbc3-7cd1a732535b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369273}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb41f199-2f33-5f68-9a53-dffe3c93ab84" :question/math-academy-id "q-124268" :question/problem "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{u}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-[(0)^{4}-(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b329fb-ed63-5c9c-ba4f-1ae88547c789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411696} {:answer/id #uuid "3565a038-62ce-5c59-8e95-c3505e80c675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411705} {:answer/id #uuid "46436b71-8e85-5a39-bf27-5ed0f753e896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411714} {:answer/id #uuid "c32ac31a-d68d-5bbe-8d37-51261f4658fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411784} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdd4223-88ec-5c0a-a0b3-1dc095b07709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbdd4223-88ec-5c0a-a0b3-1dc095b07709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411816} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed96f7f-49a8-554d-a91d-a5525992b220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411829}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58242fe6-4f94-5872-a2af-27a25e52256a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124278" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d83f771aa650879909c33d7033e80ca044b7e6f8369e19667e6173aa8a40737.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x+\\sqrt{3y})\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(0,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2+t(0-2)),\\,(0+t(3-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{3y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2-2t)+\\sqrt{3(3t)} \\\\  & =2-2t+3\\sqrt{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3{t}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{[2t-{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3[(2(1)-(1)^{2}+2(1)^{3/2})-(0)] \\\\  & =3(3) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166696 :question/id #uuid "6ddfa412-1f5a-5ddd-8d61-d7f27b106022" :question/math-academy-id "e-8751" :question/problem "For which vector field is $f(x,y) = \\sin(2x-y)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $\\mathbf F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = 2\\cos(2x-y)\\,\\mathbf i -\\cos(2x-y) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd281121-21d3-5dc4-8537-af248d0fdf46" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050885 :topic/math-academy-id 3692 :topic/title "Gradient Vector Fields"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b69b16c0-aa13-5778-b2ef-bc583efe6b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y + 1)e^{xy}i + xe^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163373} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3d328d-a7a7-526c-bbd9-76e4717393d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}e^{xy}i + x^{2}e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163374} {:answer/id #uuid "c78bec72-c383-5d7e-b9a0-8f4e09c5580e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xye^{xy}i + (x + 1)e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163375} {:answer/id #uuid "d55039d7-cd35-55e4-ad8f-f14b8bdfbbc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2}e^{xy}i + (xy + 1)e^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163376} {:answer/id #uuid "1a8f477f-887d-5565-aa72-281d9f2765d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ye^{xy}i + xye^{xy}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163377}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fa74a3cb-2703-5e63-b05a-f75c6855f983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163372} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad573080-12c0-5795-bd57-186bf4ef86e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386077} {:answer/id #uuid "6637e684-fc51-5e28-9ef7-e989f843fa89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy{e}^{xy}\\,i+(x+1){e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386078} {:answer/id #uuid "bde9cf33-16b6-581e-a442-aa5532c2cea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386079} {:answer/id #uuid "c7adf677-b962-5e7e-8a2d-2c8fa74b77c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1){e}^{xy}\\,i+x{e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386080} {:answer/id #uuid "8933c795-8d32-5777-bb3b-9769cea078c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y{e}^{xy}\\,i+xy{e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad573080-12c0-5795-bd57-186bf4ef86e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386077} :answer-field/id #uuid "65e92d39-741b-5c6a-8be4-edf3e06fa576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386076}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce34f68f-f91f-5032-a961-dafacf6d59a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104399" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})\\,j \\\\  & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\,i+(xy+1){e}^{xy}\\,j.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88f81d2d-d5c5-56a4-9386-45b306a54421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyzi + x^{2}zj + (x^{2}y + 2z)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166455} {:answer/id #uuid "114c77c1-eeaa-5774-b9a8-841df8fb3176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2}yzi + x^{2}yj - (2xy + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166456} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce24d24-00cc-5dfa-ae57-7b576ed54e17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xyzi - y^{2}zj + (y^{2}x + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166457} {:answer/id #uuid "3375422a-0420-5431-9e77-b5991e7f6f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyzi - x^{2}yj + (xz^{2} + 2y)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166458} {:answer/id #uuid "3658104d-2b4f-5a06-af82-d41092238392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyzi - x^{2}j + 4z^{2}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166459}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ee6962-d399-5885-8c69-5ff1918c56ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1fa5bbb-0cad-5524-8a00-80b3cc811832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xyz\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j+4{z}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395571} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e38753-dc36-5e53-ae48-e8e105ddc9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-xyz\\,i-{y}^{2}z\\,j+({y}^{2}x+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395572} {:answer/id #uuid "ce9c13bd-298a-540b-842f-319d1b0d07b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395573} {:answer/id #uuid "00fabcdd-6a3b-5644-9c42-6c00944bb040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}yz\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j-(2xy+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395574} {:answer/id #uuid "8d53317e-4f51-5b33-8e38-81c2f63e228f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz\\,i-{x}^{2}y\\,j+(x{z}^{2}+2y)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce9c13bd-298a-540b-842f-319d1b0d07b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395573} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c66c02b-c2b6-50bd-9cb2-f45f0ace8800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395570}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e4d7c82-2e83-562b-b6bc-3ec6c276e93c" :question/math-academy-id "q-98947" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2xyz\\,i+{x}^{2}z\\,j+({x}^{2}y+2z)\\,k.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c79e65f-4657-574d-9b28-b9892ee40b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238293} {:answer/id #uuid "7eceeb7e-4643-589d-8a42-a35eb4275ec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i+y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239158} {:answer/id #uuid "8baa56bb-b0ab-5fd3-9d83-4ae38a0f0cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239266} {:answer/id #uuid "a8db757c-0e28-5da6-9dc7-d0eddb5e601d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239527} {:answer/id #uuid "c5cda0b4-4f82-58cb-b44a-ebd0359b68ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5cda0b4-4f82-58cb-b44a-ebd0359b68ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239796} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0577431-9e1f-5827-b110-3279dc00b9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240724}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f3ac565-e5b1-59a8-9f54-0c7e96f05c2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104396" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2x\\,i-j.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45db7d37-257e-5741-9767-a2ee72bc303f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238662} {:answer/id #uuid "a026c4f1-65e8-584a-81f8-49d09aed3845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239453} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b3803d-0406-5f4c-b4f7-02086d600d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239500} {:answer/id #uuid "b49e4fa1-e304-508a-b582-63a15e87c038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239636} {:answer/id #uuid "c17f4964-aa3f-54ce-b990-6a3cc4bcb36c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6b3803d-0406-5f4c-b4f7-02086d600d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239500} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dcac30f-063e-5f3e-b8eb-a855b3db8035" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240530}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "139d3292-e475-5138-868b-b4cb7235237f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104397" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271a1e1f-1ff6-50b3-b5d4-c1267966fd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238393} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf4b57e-a5d8-5197-87f7-4d37497177cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238584} {:answer/id #uuid "4fcb3d5f-1df2-530a-a137-6eb6c29edfb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238741} {:answer/id #uuid "602128e3-6a58-54b5-9c00-03a9dc5cebe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238897} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfb84d9-8417-5a4e-acd5-04777c4b55e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddfb84d9-8417-5a4e-acd5-04777c4b55e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239992} :answer-field/id #uuid "95d8cc0b-f8f2-5892-9930-6c06feb6852d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240594}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbb95ba6-f378-5cdd-befa-8f5b533b38d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104398" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3y))\\,j \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,i-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x-3y)\\,j.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89279b6a-4252-5259-b05c-c3d2ef4f194f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382684} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae9b086-4ca3-5355-af06-586613fd1dc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,i-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382685} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1ed37a-ed72-5634-8413-f5aedf5da794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382686} {:answer/id #uuid "6d47ca13-b171-5cc5-9de1-aa1e0daf4499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i-\\frac{xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382687} {:answer/id #uuid "5b384027-61ed-5223-9ea6-1b096f29bda4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b384027-61ed-5223-9ea6-1b096f29bda4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382688} :answer-field/id #uuid "5323b306-b747-5d4a-8438-95e5a038dfe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382683}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a92703b-4103-5d43-a169-46ae386c0c50" :question/math-academy-id "q-104400" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7109fa2b-c389-5fc6-b0c4-b41d4d74bf4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\,i-y\\,j-4z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382691} {:answer/id #uuid "e9db5c91-f335-58aa-97c3-99952996d70f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-2y\\,j-2z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382692} {:answer/id #uuid "0631570a-280c-5f08-a196-767cab352a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\,i-2y\\,j-4z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382693} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7f9857-e151-5110-9b90-a5c8c453d023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\,i+2y\\,j+4z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382694} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc25693-095f-5e5b-8994-90d409ec8817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x\\,i-y\\,j-2z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0631570a-280c-5f08-a196-767cab352a76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\,i-2y\\,j-4z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382693} :answer-field/id #uuid "777faee0-1b4e-5ed3-9d6b-efa0173e6d16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382690}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99ded14c-42f7-5e12-a882-b682276e471b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104402" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =6x\\,i-2y\\,j-4z\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=6x\\,i-2y\\,j-4z\\,k.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7a94cea-936d-5e4d-ae19-8ad4604dbaa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,j-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382698} {:answer/id #uuid "e3feb8c8-b6b5-5b0c-979a-fa204c7f4e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382699} {:answer/id #uuid "115abe44-c0ca-57da-b518-6f2d0f2c33ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-z)\\,i-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382700} {:answer/id #uuid "45dbde3e-c0f4-522e-8450-38dd964b2722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,i-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382701} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a258a7-72bb-5a84-a2bc-356a3c11b847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,i+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-z)\\,j-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3feb8c8-b6b5-5b0c-979a-fa204c7f4e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382699} :answer-field/id #uuid "52aae08c-2f4f-5759-bdb7-0ce12352bc22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382697}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92017542-0c54-511c-9493-08cbabacf96f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104403" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-z)$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-z))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-z))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy-z))\\,k \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy-z)\\,k.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4316cd9b-1517-5a38-89bb-fe261972cb80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382705} {:answer/id #uuid "c74231a7-4c08-5c80-aad4-2f6813ba5103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i-j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382706} {:answer/id #uuid "e4527c6a-2fc5-5c2f-b8da-1f12673c53d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,i+{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,j+{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382707} {:answer/id #uuid "822aa67f-98a4-57c6-8772-9ee5aa70cf3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,i-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,j-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382708} {:answer/id #uuid "22eacead-e6b0-5320-9bdc-0ffab1b473ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,i-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,j-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "822aa67f-98a4-57c6-8772-9ee5aa70cf3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,i-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,j-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382708} :answer-field/id #uuid "98bee3aa-ab3e-511a-89b6-caa042a74ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382704}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d18a5537-9a57-5262-bad7-d34e0ce877ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-104404" :question/problem "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y,z)={e}^{2x-y-z}$ a potential function?" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-y-z})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-y-z})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2x-y-z})\\,k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,i-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,j-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,k \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=2{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,i-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,j-{e}^{2x-y-z}\\,k.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vector Field Corresponding to a Given Potential Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166864 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166717 :question/id #uuid "ae01904d-13f0-5d6e-ad3a-cc714c65765b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9500" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\arctan{3x} \\, \\mathbf j$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i+ Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j$ be a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ the domain $D = \\mathbb R^2,$ and we have\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{2ay}{1+9x^2}, \\qquad Q = \\arctan{3x}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{2a}{1 + 9x^2}, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{3}{1 + 9x^2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on $\\mathbb R^2.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a}{1+9{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{1+9{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2a & =3 \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = \\dfrac{3}{2}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ad87b50-24eb-5866-a7a3-616f4089f740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163379} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d86d76-1ed0-5562-a0b6-7e81a5bdd6a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163380} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a7e19-8534-5440-a04f-59429a4c1084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163381} {:answer/id #uuid "1bcf0893-e2c6-53b9-ac36-110666bdc913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163382} {:answer/id #uuid "62273ef4-b30a-5ac7-bfb0-b7ed3c358094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163383}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2cd8df0e-a91d-558b-bb05-ba85ed8ba7d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163378} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5cc30dd-c54c-510e-a8ae-905a8e5f5570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382711} {:answer/id #uuid "c34ca81d-7ee1-541d-b3dd-2ca1a9a7b794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382712} {:answer/id #uuid "ed208e02-eb59-5dac-832b-a002914d41da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382713} {:answer/id #uuid "6f96d04c-ea2f-566b-890a-1654a4e46ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382714} {:answer/id #uuid "6b74b360-5da5-5315-8da6-0afc30df25d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f96d04c-ea2f-566b-890a-1654a4e46ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382714} :answer-field/id #uuid "09bd8799-4e53-54b3-b672-a9390042cbde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382710}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2658d9bc-59d5-580d-bbeb-6f2b334d98b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-104412" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(x-a){e}^{x+y}\\,i+x{e}^{x+y}\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,Q=x{e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=(x-a){e}^{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=(x+1){e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a){e}^{x+y} & =(x+1){e}^{x+y} \\\\ (x-a){e}^{x+y}-(x+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ (x-a-x-1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ -(a+1){e}^{x+y} & =0 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bfb852c-7d91-5cab-a4e2-b111d25e0b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256899} {:answer/id #uuid "7e881c79-6475-5588-8be9-f6821d563a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257907} {:answer/id #uuid "895f93e5-2793-5091-8d7e-8ac3ac6cb993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257988} {:answer/id #uuid "d20f891c-8cea-5f52-ae6b-7335a42bce68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258612} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0483eb-6d10-563a-b7b9-8fbebc7e5b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff0483eb-6d10-563a-b7b9-8fbebc7e5b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259022} :answer-field/id #uuid "41941692-41a8-5fa2-93ae-ce67bff52ff4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259151}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86789f73-1843-5bdb-8f6f-64ce79172176" :question/math-academy-id "q-123996" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=2ay\\,i+(3-x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=2ay,\\,Q=3-x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a & =-1 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ca1b15-c83a-5a5b-99dd-ef8fece2b7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52ca1b15-c83a-5a5b-99dd-ef8fece2b7ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234674} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eab04f3-72d0-5700-818d-000286df1e51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235000}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cc69a41-78c7-5072-a175-2938b84fa694" :question/math-academy-id "q-168600" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n>$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor our vector field $F,$ the domain is $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=a{y}^{3}-1,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3a{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a{y}^{2} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3a & =3 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e828d95-ecbc-57d8-b8d9-acf763b088e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238462} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6e3eea-6dbc-567a-b7c6-ba10a5fed190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238572} {:answer/id #uuid "541fb43b-1bee-5827-afeb-bd3595f7ffbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238779} {:answer/id #uuid "7802141d-b50b-5e83-be03-15a439e33fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239092} {:answer/id #uuid "a8355852-7497-53ad-930e-a02821cc5439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7802141d-b50b-5e83-be03-15a439e33fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239092} :answer-field/id #uuid "816ba5d9-4c22-58e9-ba7c-700eb48c8a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240560}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f05e7a0d-26f2-588a-8d78-a6a57d732300" :question/math-academy-id "q-104410" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(ay-2x{y}^{3})\\,i+(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ we have $P=ay-2x{y}^{3}$ and $Q=x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=a-6x{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1-6x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-6x{y}^{2} & =1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1bc900-62d1-5615-af0f-6dbe4ee35a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238300} {:answer/id #uuid "2899ff60-966e-5c9f-a77b-cd076462b487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238412} {:answer/id #uuid "501fc527-192e-54f2-86ab-52b7b2842115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238747} {:answer/id #uuid "dac6fd53-9350-56e6-9302-b6846604e6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239963} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b1e404-5480-5ec1-86f2-cbf9a0ee1d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2899ff60-966e-5c9f-a77b-cd076462b487" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238412} :answer-field/id #uuid "f74e5e09-607e-5411-a367-f75da43c0068" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240766}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7bd6d6aa-1b16-5a93-a052-cdf54093004e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104414" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=\\frac{axy}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{axy}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ax}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ ax & =2x \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57190c25-01e9-52f1-a602-8b6b3c0b0030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257533} {:answer/id #uuid "7dfeddeb-7431-5e49-a9dd-7f38f3f510f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257898} {:answer/id #uuid "83f96867-7578-5dde-bcc0-b2a067859fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257951} {:answer/id #uuid "e808abd0-4e4a-50c9-b1de-e375e94dc248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258794} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0ebf91-bb0c-5cda-9ad5-c786f1c45f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef0ebf91-bb0c-5cda-9ad5-c786f1c45f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258855} :answer-field/id #uuid "df48032b-0934-5e84-9809-b7bc5cdb5be7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259425}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7768381-49c0-5755-a526-0e09cbb087b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-123993" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=({y}^{3}-ay)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-2x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP={y}^{3}-ay,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}-2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-a & =3{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ -a & =-2 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14db70f5-df8f-5f08-a150-3a466567a842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256971} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb1fb64-ec36-5925-bee7-1a5e8272a7ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257201} {:answer/id #uuid "61f28b33-098c-5f5d-9ab0-c30766af1628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257644} {:answer/id #uuid "7533dde9-b1e2-58c9-81d7-b484f86dcbda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257810} {:answer/id #uuid "9813db15-8d6c-5d3c-b904-d7c6bd10d9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9813db15-8d6c-5d3c-b904-d7c6bd10d9bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258123} :answer-field/id #uuid "272f8a51-e7d1-5e59-aadf-b74351e207b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259099}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca502a8a-0b55-5b4d-8a4b-58f7420e37da" :question/math-academy-id "q-123998" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=({y}^{2}+2xy)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2axy)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP={y}^{2}+2xy,\\,Q={x}^{2}+2axy.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y+2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+2ay.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+2x & =2x+2ay \\\\ 2y & =2ay \\\\ 2y-2ay & =0 \\\\ 2y(1-a) & =0 \\\\ 1-a & =0 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259583 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f02a01-13bc-56b7-b177-ccb430449d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256871} {:answer/id #uuid "27841610-a8b4-5bab-82b2-27e5466d5850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257130} {:answer/id #uuid "470598a5-e320-5126-88c8-0994c3641e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257403} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aadeb0-045b-5aca-85eb-0efc92490277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258337} {:answer/id #uuid "c1adda1d-9da5-5d62-bd41-e44c2f642fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09f02a01-13bc-56b7-b177-ccb430449d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256871} :answer-field/id #uuid "def33358-ee55-5273-82f4-3c6232c5e848" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259424}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "652885e3-f092-5423-886c-a33306bd20ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-124000" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(ax{y}^{2}+1)\\,i+(4{x}^{2}y+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=ax{y}^{2}+1,\\,Q=4{x}^{2}y+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2axy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8xy.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2axy & =8xy \\\\ 2axy-8xy & =0 \\\\ 2(a-4)xy & =0 \\\\ a-4 & =0 \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the last equality must be satisfied everywhere on $D,$ we conclude that $a-4=0,$ that is, $a=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4568d555-f227-5e34-a46e-e128971708e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361137} {:answer/id #uuid "62339377-95b5-5286-afc6-4c2f3145b84b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361276} {:answer/id #uuid "79919e77-489f-5457-a3b0-ecf7191bb0b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361372} {:answer/id #uuid "bb81ef3b-a990-5f38-bd1a-392f516c4a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361674} {:answer/id #uuid "e92122ee-410e-5b80-a54b-ad31ed1ba6c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4568d555-f227-5e34-a46e-e128971708e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361137} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d7cb4af-cadc-59d2-b331-ed625ffc95bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361990}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4992c928-66f9-55e4-9096-4efa99debc55" :question/math-academy-id "q-98946" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=(3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y)\\,i+(ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y,\\,Q=ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y & =a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd9d3890-e5fa-57a3-9137-eeba2c84a593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383057} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e62bfe-1108-53de-82a5-a03533c4f994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383058} {:answer/id #uuid "d05e703d-f6df-5364-a2c7-03adc5fdd414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383059} {:answer/id #uuid "31c18c30-748a-5d6c-9a1a-4722fa3a251d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383060} {:answer/id #uuid "70ad12cb-bd4a-5ceb-a9c2-f1f68db1330e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31c18c30-748a-5d6c-9a1a-4722fa3a251d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383060} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bcb7611-75f0-57b9-8afb-f4a5ccb4b065" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383056}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2d723a2-0f08-5c17-a7db-01dafeefa476" :question/math-academy-id "q-123999" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=a\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=a\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y),\\,Q=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-y)+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-a\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y).\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-a\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y)+a\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & =0 \\\\ (3+a)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-y) & =0 \\\\ 3+a & =0 \\\\ a & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-3.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parameter in a Given Conservative Vector Field"}]}
